# KeyValueRow — a name on the left, its value hard against the right edge, on one
# line. The summary shape: an order total, a usage panel, the facts down the side
# of a detail page.
#
#     use KeyValueList():
#         use KeyValueRow("Seats", "12")
#         use KeyValueRow("Rate", "$8 / seat / month")
#         use KeyValueTotal("Due today", "$96.00")
#
# ── WHY THIS IS NOT `SpecRow`, WHICH IS ALSO A KEY AND A VALUE ─────────────────
#
# They are two different alignments and each is wrong where the other is right.
# `Specs` puts the keys in a COLUMN — a fixed measure on the left, values starting
# at the same x down the whole block — which is what makes a reference table
# scannable and what makes a two-item summary look like a table with a hole in it.
# This puts the key and the value at opposite ENDS of one line, which is what a
# receipt does, and which falls apart the moment there are fifteen rows and the
# eye has nothing to track. Rule of thumb: reading down a list of facts is
# `Specs`; adding up a short list of numbers is this.
#
# THERE ARE NO SEPARATOR HAIRLINES BETWEEN THE ROWS, and that is a limit stated
# rather than a taste. The obvious rule is `.x-kv + .x-kv { border-top: … }`, and
# it does not work here: a `use` renders a wrapper element around the component's
# nodes, so two rows written as two `use` calls are never DOM siblings and the
# adjacent-sibling selector matches nothing. (The same rule in `ui/specs.fct` has
# the same problem, for the same reason.) What DOES work is a rule that needs no
# sibling — so the one line this shape actually needs, the rule above a total, is
# a component of its own, and the rows in between are separated by space.
#
# `KeyValueTotal` is that row: the same line, heavier, with a rule above it. It is
# a second component rather than an `emphasis: bool` because the border is the
# whole difference and a boolean that draws a line is a boolean nobody remembers
# the direction of.
app FacetData:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-kv-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px;
                      min-width: 0; border: none; background: none; padding: 0; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-kv { display: flex; flex-direction: row; align-items: baseline;
                 justify-content: space-between; gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap;
                 border: none; background: none; padding: 0; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-kv-k { color: var(--fa-muted); font-size: 14px; }
        # `min-width: 0` and a right-hand text alignment, so a long value wraps
        # under itself rather than pushing the key off the row.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-kv-v { font-size: 14px; text-align: right; min-width: 0;
                 font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-kv-total { border-top: 1px solid var(--x-line, #2f3336);
                      padding-top: 10px; margin-top: 2px; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-kv-total .x-kv-k { color: var(--fa-fg); font-weight: 700;
                              font-size: 15px; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-kv-total .x-kv-v { font-weight: 800; font-size: 17px; }
    component KeyValueList():
        box class "x-kv-list":
            slot
    component KeyValueRow(key: text, value: text):
        row class "x-kv":
            text "{key}" class "x-kv-k"
            text "{value}" class "x-kv-v"
    component KeyValueTotal(key: text, value: text):
        row class "x-kv x-kv-total":
            text "{key}" class "x-kv-k"
            text "{value}" class "x-kv-v"
